8233277,2024-01-27,Debt collection,I do not know,Threatened to contact someone or share information improperly,Contacted you after you asked them to stop,"Midland Credit Management ( heretofore MCM ) is wilfully disregarding a legal opt-out request per 15 U.S.C. 1692c ( c ). MCM acknowledge the receipt of said opt-out request in their latest letter to me dated XX/XX/2024, in which they attempt to suggest that their communication attempt is legal. This is false, as I have requested ( and my request was granted ) not to be contacted, and as such MCM can not legally notify me that they are merely considering forwarding their account to their attorney. For the avoidance of doubt : I have previously opted out of receiving communications from them, and they acknowledge this fact in the first and the last sentence of their letter. Combined, these indicate to any and all reasonable observers that they are clearly acting in a wilfully negligent manner. Specifically, this sort of communication which they sent me is strictly prohibited by the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ), specifically section 1692c ( c ). Here 's why : 1. Opt-out right : The FDCPA grants consumers the right to stop receiving communications from debt collectors. This right applies to all forms of communication, including phone calls, letters, emails, and text messages.

2. Communication after opt-out : Once a consumer has opted out, the debt collector is generally prohibited from contacting them directly. This includes any communication about the debt, even if it is intended to inform the consumer about potential legal action.

3. Exceptions : There are a few narrow exceptions to the prohibition on communication after opt-out. For example, a debt collector may still contact the consumer to : ( a ) Correct or update information about the debt ; ( b ) Offer a settlement or repayment plan ; ( c ) Inform the consumer about specific legal actions, such as filing a lawsuit or repossessing property. However, simply notifying the consumer that their account may be forwarded to an attorney does not fall within any of these exceptions.

Therefore, since they received an opt-out request from me, they should have ceased all communication with me unless it falls within one of the narrow exceptions permitted by the FDCPA.",,ENCORE CAPITAL GROUP INC.,VA,223XX,,Web,2024-01-27,Closed with non-monetary relief,Yes
